  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 122 PM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nMore widespread rainfall has occurred from southern Wisconsin \nsouthward in association with a well-developed MCV. Locally, \nthere are small (20-30%) chances for a shower this afternoon, \nbut limited instability above the -10C level should keep any\nlightning fairly isolated. \n\nThe period of most interest remains Friday night into Saturday. \nAnomalous moisture lifts northward with precipitable water values \nexceeding the 95th percentile relative to climo. A few \nshortwave troughs will interact with the moist, unstable airmass\nand low-level baroclinic zone to produce periods of \nshowers/storms. Confidence in timing/placement of convection \ntends to be in low in these set-ups, especially beyond a day or \ntwo. The greater instability will generally remain south of the \narea with mean low-level jet placement also focused south. Thus,\nthe risk for severe storms is low and the higher odds of \nheavier rain remain just to the south. However, if there is \nsufficient instability, given the moisture present, any storms \nmay pose a heavy rain threat with NBM 90th percentile rain \namounts in the 2 to 2.5 inches across central into east-central \nWisconsin on Saturday. These details will be fine-tuned over the\nnext day.\n\nBeyond this time frame, high pressure will build south to start next \nweek. A shortwave trough will drive a cold front south across the \narea toward Tuesday. Although the greatest instability looks to \nremain southwest of the area, there is some overlap in \nCAPE/shear evident in global ensembles, owing to the continued \npresence of seasonably strong mid-level flow. Thus, although \nconfidence in severe weather is low, the risk for showers and \nstorms increases to 50-70% later Tuesday into Tuesday night. \n\nFairly seasonable temperatures will persist over the coming week \nwith highs in the 70s and 80s.",
